  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>2.3.3.2 Demonstrate x^4 many-to-one</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p class="tb-exercise">Exercise</p>
<p>Show that \(f(x) = x^4\) is a many-to-one function. Give an example of two different inputs \(x_1\) and \(x_2\) that have the same output, and then write down the two outputs.</p>
<div>\(x_1 =\) [[input:ansx1]] [[validation:ansx1]]</div>
<div>\(x_2 =\) [[input:ansx2]] [[validation:ansx2]]</div>
<div>\(y_1 = f(x_1) =\) [[input:ansy1]] [[validation:ansy1]]</div>
<div>\(y_2 = f(x_2) =\) [[input:ansy2]] [[validation:ansy2]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A function is <strong>many-to-one</strong> when two different inputs give the same output. For an even power, changing the sign of \(x\) does not change the value, because \((-x)^4 = x^4\).</p>
<p>For example, take \(x_1 = {@tax1@}\) and \(x_2 = {@tax2@}\). These inputs are different, but</p>
<p>\[ f({@tax1@}) = {@tax1@}^4 = {@tay1@} \quad \text{and} \quad f({@tax2@}) = ({@tax2@})^4 = {@tay1@}. \]</p>
<p>So \(y_1 = y_2 = {@tay1@}\): two different inputs share one output, and \(f(x) = x^4\) is many-to-one. Any pair \(a\) and \(-a\) with \(a \ne 0\) works just as well.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
